Prectical Explanation

In this project, I used the Scapy library to capture live network packets. When the program starts, it first displays all the available network interfaces.
interfaces = get_if_list()

The user selects the desired network interface.
choice = int(input("Select Interface Number: "))
selected_iface = interfaces[choice - 1]

Next, the user selects the protocol (TCP, UDP, ICMP, or ALL).
protocol = input("Enter Protocol (TCP/UDP/ICMP/ALL): ").upper()

The program then captures packets using Scapy's sniff() function.
sniff(
    iface=selected_iface,
    lfilter=packet_filter,
    prn=packet_callback,
    store=False
)

For every captured packet, the packet_callback() function is executed.
packet_callback(packet)

Inside this function, the packet is analyzed and its summary is displayed.
print(packet.summary())

For example, the output shows:
Ether / IPv6 / TCP
This means:
•	Ether → Data Link Layer 
•	IPv6 → Network Layer 
•	TCP → Transport Layer 
By analyzing these layers, we can understand the structure and content of each network packet.

